    "id": "36dd0f26-3064-468a-8e0c-ff4d390b30b0"
  },
  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.341292",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "Most people think moving toward software engineering is an upgrade.\nI thought that too.\n\n\nUntil I realized I wasn\u2019t becoming a \u201cbetter\u201d engineer, I was just stepping into a system with different constraints.\n\nIn data engineering, I optimized for correctness and scale.\nIn software systems, I had to think about failure modes, latency, and user impact in ways I had never considered before.\n\nSame fundamentals.\nDifferent trade-offs.\n\nThis issue breaks down what actually changes when you move closer to software engineering, and why thinking in terms of hierarchy will slow you down. If you\u2019re navigating this transition, this one will recalibrate how you see your own experience.\n\nHave you ever felt like you were \u201cstarting over\u201d\u2026 only to realize later you weren\u2019t?\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 771,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "0",
        "comments": "0",
        "shares": "0"
      },
      "original_timestamp": ""
    },
    "metrics": {
      "likes": 0,
      "comments": 0
    },
    "profile_id": "haymang",
    "id": "3f4c1944-6193-4f89-be50-52bbabaf583d"
  },
  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.347330",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "Most engineers focus on learning new tools.\nSpark.\nAirflow.\nKafka.\n\n\nBut when I joined Amazon, I realized something surprising.\n\nThe biggest shift wasn\u2019t technology. It was the questions engineers ask before writing code.\n\nQuestions like:\n- What happens if this pipeline fails at 2 AM?\n- What if upstream data arrives late?\n- How much will this job cost every month?\n- Which downstream systems break if this fails?\n\nAt small scale, you focus on making things work.\nAt large scale, you focus on what happens when things break.\n\nThat shift changed how I think about building systems.\nIn Issue #7 of No Reset Required, I write about:\n* Designing for failure\n* Operational ownership\n* Why data quality becomes a system problem\n* And how scale changes engineering judgment\n\nIf you're early in your data or engineering career, this mindset shift is one of the most important ones.\nIssue #7 is live.\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 898,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "0",
        "comments": "0",
        "shares": "0"
      },
      "original_timestamp": ""
    },
    "metrics": {
      "likes": 0,
      "comments": 0
    },
    "profile_id": "haymang",
    "id": "afe2ef96-0456-428e-81a0-d0917789aa75"
  },
  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.353923",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "Most engineers think the hardest part of big tech is the technology.\nIt isn\u2019t.\n\n\nThe first thing that surprised me at Amazon wasn\u2019t the scale.\nIt was the writing.\n\nThree weeks into the job, I was asked to write a design document for a Spark ETL pipeline.\nNot just what it would do, but:\n- how long it would run\n- what resources it would consume\n- how much it would cost every month\n- and why the system needed it at all\n\nAt first, it felt excessive.\nOnly later did I realize what this process was really doing.\nIt forced engineers to think clearly before writing code.\nAnd at Amazon scale, unclear thinking becomes expensive.\n\nThis issue explores how Amazon\u2019s writing culture reshaped how I design systems.\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 712,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "0",
        "comments": "0",
        "shares": "0"
      },
      "original_timestamp": ""
    },
    "metrics": {
      "likes": 0,
      "comments": 0
    },
    "profile_id": "haymang",
    "id": "ec93493c-8682-4ed7-bc68-be0b749d32a7"
  },
  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.359458",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "You can grow for years\u2026 and still stay at the same level.\n\n\nThat realization hit me when everything at work looked \u201cfine.\u201d\nI had moved from Talend to building Spark ETL pipelines from scratch. I was tuning distributed jobs, handling performance issues, and working on real production systems.\n\nOn paper, it looked like growth.\nBut the learning curve had flattened.\nAnd I started asking a difficult question:\nWas I growing, or just becoming efficient at the same complexity?\n\nThat question eventually pulled me out of a stable environment and into a startup, where batch systems met real-time GPS data pipelines \u2014 and comfort disappeared.\nThis issue is about:\n\u2022 When comfort becomes expensive\n \u2022 Why environment compounds faster than titles\n \u2022 And how choosing depth over safety changed my trajectory\nIssue #5 is live. Link in the comments section.\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 853,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "0",
        "comments": "1",
        "shares": "0"
      },
      "original_timestamp": ""
    },
    "metrics": {
      "likes": 0,
      "comments": 1
