    "profile_id": "haymang",
    "id": "2e3c7ade-225d-483e-93fb-0520e00a7dd2"
  },
  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.365142",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "We celebrate optimization too early.\n\nFaster queries.\nLower latency.\nCleaner code.\nIt feels like progress.\n\nBut in production systems, speed is rarely what breaks trust.\nFragility does.\n\nIn this next issue: Transitioning Without Reset, I wrote about something I had to learn the hard way working at Amazon.\n\nAn optimized system can still be unreliable.\nAnd unreliable systems fail quietly.\n\nThis one is about the mental shift from performance-first thinking to resilience-first design, especially if you're moving from data systems into product-facing engineering.\n\nIf you\u2019ve ever optimized something that later caused pain in production, this will resonate.\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 664,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "0",
        "comments": "0",
