  },
  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.380435",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "Correctness vs Consequences\n\nFor years, I thought good engineering meant writing correct code.\n\nAs a data engineer, that mental model worked.\n\nPipelines had retries.\nJobs had rollbacks.\nFailures were visible.\nIf something broke, it turned red.\n\nThe system told you.\n\nThen I started working closer to product-facing systems.\n\nAnd I realized something uncomfortable:\n\nIn software systems, things don\u2019t always fail loudly.\n\nThey fail partially.\n\nA request succeeds\u2026 but writes inconsistent state.\nA retry works\u2026 but duplicates side effects.\nA deployment passes tests\u2026 but degrades latency quietly.\n\nNothing is red.\nBut something is wrong.\n\nThat\u2019s when my mental model shifted:\n\nData engineering often optimizes for correct outputs over time.\nSoftware systems optimize for state integrity under continuous change.\n\nCorrectness is necessary.\n\nBut consequences are what matter.\n\nNow, before merging code, I ask:\n\n\u201cWhat happens if this runs twice?\u201d\n\u201cWhat happens if this times out?\u201d\n\u201cWhat happens if only half of this succeeds?\u201d\n\nThat shift changed how I design everything.\n\nIf you\u2019re transitioning from DE to SDE (or vice versa), when did you start thinking in failure paths instead of happy paths?\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 1198,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "0",
        "comments": "2",
        "shares": "0"
      },
      "original_timestamp": ""
    },
    "metrics": {
      "likes": 0,
      "comments": 2
    },
    "profile_id": "haymang",
    "id": "13b28e47-4b67-4e8e-a5a9-71db7eb17544"
  },
  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.386447",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "I once interviewed for a senior role and got rejected.\n\nThe next day, the same company called me back and offered a role one level lower.\n\nSame skills. Same effort. Different outcome.\n\nThat\u2019s when I realized something most career advice never talks about:\neffort isn\u2019t scored fairly during transitions.\n\nIn the latest issue of No Reset Required, I write about misaligned scorecards, quiet progress, and why working harder doesn\u2019t always move you forward.\n\nIf you\u2019re putting in the work but not seeing results, this one might explain why.\n\n\ud83d\udc49 Subscribe to the newsletter\n\u2026more",
