  },
  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.438052",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "You\u2019ve been using this software design principle\u2026 without knowing its name\n\nEver refreshed a page.\nRetried an API call.\nRe-ran a failed job.\n\nAnd nothing broke.\n\nThat\u2019s idempotency at work.\n\nMost engineers use it long before they learn it.\n\nWhat it means (in plain English):\nDoing the same operation multiple times should have the same result as doing it once.\n\n. . . .\n\nWhere it hides in plain sight\n\n\u2022 Clicking \u201cSave\u201d twice doesn\u2019t duplicate data\n\u2022 Retrying a payment doesn\u2019t double-charge\n\u2022 Reprocessing yesterday\u2019s data doesn\u2019t corrupt tables\n\u2022 Deploying the same infra twice doesn\u2019t recreate resources\n\nYou trust systems because someone designed them to be idempotent.\n\n. . . .\n\nWhy this matters more than people realize\n\nReal systems fail.\nNetworks timeout.\nJobs retry.\nUsers double-click.\n\nIf your system isn\u2019t idempotent:\n\u2192 retries become bugs\n\u2192 failures become data corruption\n\u2192 scale becomes fear\n\nIdempotency is what turns \u201cretry\u201d from a risk into a feature.\n\n. . . . \n\nThe quiet shift engineers go through\n\nEarly career:\n\n\u201cWhy did this run twice?\u201d\n\nLater:\n\n\u201cAssume it will run twice.\u201d\n\nThat mental shift is what separates scripts from systems.\n\n. . . .\n\nWhere this shows up across roles\n\n\u2022 SDEs \u2192 APIs, payments, infra, deployments\n\u2022 Data Engineers \u2192 backfills, reprocessing, streaming jobs\n\u2022 Platform teams \u2192 reliability at scale\n\nDifferent domains. Same principle.\n\n. . . . \n\nThe best part?\n\nYou don\u2019t add idempotency after things break.\nYou design as if things will break.\n\nThat\u2019s real engineering.\n\n. . . . \n\nWhat\u2019s a software concept you used for years before you realized it even had a name?\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 1615,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "0",
