  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.443952",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "I didn\u2019t \u201cget\u201d Data Vault at first. Then I saw it like software.\n\nWhen I first came across Data Vault, it felt\u2026 overengineered.\nHubs, Links, Satellites.\nExtra tables. Extra joins. Extra thinking.\n\nMy instinct was: \u201cWhy not just model the data cleanly and move on?\u201d\n\nThen something clicked.\n\nData Vault isn\u2019t a data concept.\nIt\u2019s a software concept in disguise.\n\nThink about it like this:\n\u2022 Hubs \u2192 Objects with identity (what)\n\u2022 Links \u2192 Relationships between objects (how they connect)\n\u2022 Satellites \u2192 State and behavior over time (how they change)\n\nSuddenly, it felt familiar.\n\nAnd once you see it that way, you realize what Data Vault is actually solving:\n\u2022 Source systems change \u2192 your core model doesn\u2019t break\n\u2022 Business rules evolve \u2192 history is preserved, not overwritten\n\u2022 Pipelines fail \u2192 reloads are deterministic and safe\n\u2022 Multiple teams ingest data \u2192 no one steps on each other\n\nJust like good software design,\nData Vault separates identity, relationships, and change.\n\nIs it always needed? No.\nIs it heavy? Yes.\nIs it powerful when data, teams, and requirements grow? Absolutely.\n\nI still don\u2019t reach for Data Vault by default.\nBut now, when complexity shows up,\nI see it as a design tool \u2014 not a modeling dogma.\n\n\nHave you actually used Data Vault in production?\nWhat problem did it genuinely solve for you \u2014 and what pain did it introduce?\n\u2026more",
