  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.484450",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "Most LLM agents work great\u2026 until you put them in production.\nThat\u2019s when the real problems show up \u2014 the ones no demo or tutorial ever mentions.\n\nLast month I built an agent to analyze our cloud infrastructure metrics.\nLooked perfect in notebooks.\nThen production happened.\n\nAnd suddenly I was dealing with things like:\n \u2022\u00a0\u00a0agents hallucinating remediation steps\n \u2022\u00a0\u00a0non-deterministic plans that changed on every retry\n \u2022\u00a0\u00a0API calls going to prod instead of staging\n \u2022\u00a0\u00a0monitoring loops that spiraled cost\n \u2022\u00a0\u00a0debugging paths that felt like reading someone else\u2019s diary\n\nNone of this was an \u201cAI problem.\u201d\nIt was an engineering problem.\n\nThat\u2019s why Amazon\u2019s new Agent Core framework caught my attention \u2014 not because it makes agents \u201csmarter,\u201d but because it makes them predictable, observable, and testable.\n\nIf you\u2019re building agents that interact with real systems, the hard part isn\u2019t reasoning\u2026\nIt\u2019s everything around reasoning.\n\nI just finished a deep dive on this:\n\ud83d\udc49 \u201cWhat Breaks When You Run Agents in Production \u2014 and How Agent Core Fixes It.\u201d\n\n\nRead the full article on Medium - link in comments section, and share your thoughts.\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 1146,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "4",
        "comments": "1",
