    "profile_id": "haymang",
    "id": "3fa1c734-21a3-404d-8ba9-5102b96e00bf"
  },
  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.503483",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "Most engineers don\u2019t fail because they lack skills \u2014 they fail because they optimize for the wrong thing.\n\n\n\nToday, I want to share one lesson that changed my career at Amazon \u2014 from BI \u2192 DE \u2192 Senior DE \u2192 Senior SDE.\n\nAs you grow in engineering, the question shifts from:\n\n\u201cCan you build this?\u201d \u2192\n\u201cCan you choose the right way to build this?\u201d\n\nThat single shift takes you from an executor to an engineer who designs systems with intention.\n\n. . . . \n\nHere are 3 trade-off skills that mattered more than anything else in my transition:\n\n1. Data Engineer \u2192 Software Engineer requires one mental upgrade:\n\nStop thinking in pipelines. Start thinking in systems.\nA pipeline processes data.\nA system survives failures, scales, observes itself, and guarantees correctness.\n\n\n2. Senior roles require this question daily:\n\nWhat\u2019s the simplest design that won\u2019t break in production?\nNot the coolest.\nNot the most \u201ccloud-native.\u201d\nNot the one with five services and six queues.\n\nThe simplest thing that works \u2014 and keeps working.\n\n\n3. Being \u201ctechnical\u201d is not enough.\n\nThe real accelerator is learning to articulate why you made a decision.\nBecause senior-level engineering = decision making + communication.\n\n. . . .\n\nAs I continue sharing DE \u2192 SDE transition lessons, I\u2019ll break down more trade-offs from real-world systems at Amazon and beyond.\n\nIf you\u2019d like posts that simplify complex concepts and help you grow as an engineer, follow along.\n\n\ud83d\udc49 Meanwhile, what\u2019s one engineering trade-off you misunderstood earlier in your career?\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 1531,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "1",
        "comments": "0",
        "shares": "0"
      },
      "original_timestamp": ""
    },
    "metrics": {
      "likes": 1,
