  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.542582",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "\ud83e\udd2f Most companies still measure developer productivity wrong \u2014 and Atlassian just called them out.\n\n\n\nFor years, productivity was measured in commits, story points, and sprint velocity.\nAtlassian just changed the game.\n\nThey acquired DX, a developer intelligence platform that analyzes pull requests, CI/CD pipelines, and AI tool usage - to surface what actually slows teams down.\n\n. . . .\n\n\ud83d\udca1 Why it matters\n\nAI tools are everywhere, but visibility isn\u2019t.\nMost teams can\u2019t answer:\n\n\u201cWhat\u2019s really making our developers more productive?\u201d\n\nThis move shows where the industry\u2019s headed - from tracking activity -> to understanding flow.\n\n. . . .\n\n\u2699\ufe0f Key takeaway for builders\n\nThe next era of productivity isn\u2019t about doing more, it\u2019s about seeing better.\nIf your team can\u2019t measure friction, you can\u2019t remove it.\n\n. . . . \n\n\ud83d\udc40 Your turn:\nHow are you measuring productivity in your team today - activity or outcomes?\n\n\nhashtag\n#EngineeringProductivity \nhashtag\n#AI \nhashtag\n#Atlassian \nhashtag\n#DeveloperTools \nhashtag\n#TechStrategy \nhashtag\n#DataDriven\n\u2026more",
