  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.562187",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "\ud83d\udea8 The AI boom is hitting a reality check, and that matters for how we build systems.\n\nIf you\u2019ve been riding the wave of \u201cAI will save everything,\u201d today\u2019s news might feel like a splash of cold water:\n\n> Asia\u2019s chip and tech stocks dropped sharply as investors question how much of the AI hype is baked into earnings. \n\n> Meanwhile, a growing number of consumers are ditching apps in favour of AI assistants that do the work for them. \n\n. . . .\n\nHere\u2019s what this means for data/AI/productivity professionals like us:\n\n1\ufe0f\u20e3 Hype doesn\u2019t equal scale.\nArchitectures that were built for the rush: heavy compute, every model, every dataset will now be judged on sustainable ROI. If your system doesn\u2019t deliver value beyond the demo, it will struggle.\n\n2\ufe0f\u20e3 Focus on the shift from \u201ctools\u201d -> \u201cagents\u201d.\nIf consumers are replacing apps with assistants, your role moves from building features to enabling decision-making. That means pipelines, context, action. Not just data ingestion.\n\n3\ufe0f\u20e3 Productivity isn\u2019t growth for its own sake.\nWhen everyone is chasing the next big AI win, the winners will be those who simplify rather than overload. Lower friction, smarter automation, less bang for budget.\n\n. . . .\n\n\ud83d\udc49 My challenge for this week:\nPick one system you\u2019re working on that is built for scale (not hype).\n \u2022 Does it solve a real business problem?\n \u2022 Is the architecture cost-effective?\n \u2022 Can you run it sustainably even if \u201cAI enthusiasm\u201d dips?\n\nIf you answer yes to all three, you\u2019re ahead of the curve.\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 1507,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "1",
