  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.575996",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "\ud83e\udde0 Why your \u201cdeep work\u201d blocks aren\u2019t working (and what to fix today)\n\nWe all block off time for focused work.\nYet many of those blocks feel unproductive.\n\nThe reason isn\u2019t just distractions.\nIt\u2019s context reset \u2014 every time you switch mindset, you lose momentum.\n\n\u2e3b\n\n\ud83d\udd0d What\u2019s happening under the hood\n \u2022 You finish writing code. Then check email. Then join a meeting.\n \u2022 That meeting ends, you inspect logs. Then open a new doc.\n \u2022 You\u2019ve jumped mental models 4-5 times in 2 hours.\nStudies show it can take ~23 minutes to return to full focus after a context shift.\n\n\u2e3b\n\n\u2705 How I protect real deep work\n 1. Define task-type blocks\n \u2022 Block \u201cCode / Build\u201d separately from \u201cReview / Debug\u201d\n \u2022 Block \u201cWrite / Design\u201d separately from \u201cRespond / Align\u201d\nMixing them blurs both outcome & speed.\n 2. Use an AI \u201csession summary\u201d at hand-off\n \u2022 At the end of a session: \u201cHere\u2019s where I left off\u201d prompt to ChatGPT or Notion AI.\n \u2022 Next time you return, paste the summary \u2192 you save the ~15-20 min mental ramp.\n 3. Buffer switch time intentionally\n \u2022 After a mental model change (e.g., analysis \u2192 coding), pause 2 minutes, stretch, breathe, refocus.\n \u2022 That small buffer resets faster than chaos.\n\n\u2e3b\n\n\ud83d\udca1 Deep work isn\u2019t about eliminating everything that\u2019s not work.\nIt\u2019s about reducing the mindset resets \u2014 fewer jumps = more clarity.\nAnd in the AI-driven engineering world, your speed isn\u2019t just technical skill\u2014it\u2019s your ability to stay in the flow.\n\n\u2e3b\n\n\ud83d\udc49 What\u2019s one mindset switch you made this week to protect focus?\n\nShare your tactic or head-off a common trap.\n\u2026more",
