  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.590099",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "\u2699\ufe0f Discipline isn\u2019t about doing more. It\u2019s about designing friction.\n\nMost people fail at consistency not because they\u2019re lazy \u2014\nbut because their system has too much friction in the wrong places.\n\nWe make focus a willpower problem.\nBut productivity is mostly an environment design problem.\n\nHere\u2019s what actually works \ud83d\udc47\n\n\u2e3b\n\n\ud83d\udd39 1. Lower friction for the \u201cgood\u201d tasks\nMake it stupidly easy to start.\n \u2022 Keep your study tab pinned.\n \u2022 Keep your notes open from last session.\n \u2022 Start your day with a \u201c1-line task.\u201d\n\nYou can\u2019t scale motivation, but you can scale momentum.\n\n\u2e3b\n\n\ud83d\udd39 2. Add friction to the \u201cbad\u201d tasks\nMake distractions just a bit annoying.\n \u2022 Log out of YouTube, instagram or whatever social media app you\u2019re glued to throughout the day. Maybe, use focus feature on your phone. \n \u2022 Turn off phone search bar.\n \u2022 Keep one workspace for deep work only.\n\nSmall barriers save huge focus.\n\n\u2e3b\n\n\ud83d\udd39 3. Automate low-value decisions\nIf you decide the same thing daily \u2014 automate it.\nAI tools help here:\n \u2022 Use Notion AI to summarize your notes before the next session.\n \u2022 Use ChatGPT to plan your next 2-hour learning sprint.\n \u2022 Use calendar blockers to pre-commit energy, not time.\n\nYou don\u2019t need more discipline.\nYou need fewer decisions that drain it.\n\n\u2e3b\n\n\ud83d\udca1 Productivity isn\u2019t about intensity \u2014 it\u2019s about architecture.\nDesign your system once so your habits run on autopilot.\n\n\nWhat\u2019s one friction you can remove (or add) this week to stay consistent?\n\u2026more",
