  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.604168",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "Amazon dropped a vector bombshell few months back \u2014 here\u2019s what it means for your AI/DE stack.\n\n\nAWS announced Amazon S3 Vectors \u2014 the first cloud object store with native vector support, promising up to 90% cost reduction for storing, uploading, and querying embeddings.\n\nHere\u2019s how I break it down (so you won\u2019t forget)\ud83d\udc47\n\n\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n\n\ud83d\udd39 1. What is S3 Vectors \u2014 in your memory\n \u2022 Bucket = Storage + Query interface \u2192 not just storing vectors, it queries them too (similarity search)\n \u2022 Vector Indexes inside buckets \u2192 each bucket can host multiple indexes (millions of vectors)\n \u2022 Tiered strategy with OpenSearch \u2192 keep \u201ccold\u201d vectors in S3, \u201chot\u201d ones in OpenSearch for faster queries\n\n\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n\n\ud83d\udd39 2. Where the LLM fits\n\nLLM (or AI Agent) doesn\u2019t store vectors \u2014 it uses them.\nFlow is:\nraw data \u2192 embedding model \u2192 vector store \u2192 retrieval \u2192 LLM reasoning\n\nWhen your model needs context (RAG, semantic search, recommendations), it pulls related chunks from your vector store.\n\n\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n\n\ud83d\udd39 3. When to use what\n\n\u2705 Use S3 Vectors when:\n \u2022 You have large archives or infrequently accessed embeddings\n \u2022 You want cost-efficient storage with sub-second query performance\n\n\u2705 Use OpenSearch / dedicated vector DB when:\n \u2022 You need high QPS (queries per second) or real-time latency\n \u2022 You rely on complex metadata filtering\n\n\u2705 Use both when:\n \u2022 You can tier data smartly: keep cold vectors in S3 Vectors, hot ones in OpenSearch\n\nExample:\nBuilding a semantic search on 10 years of customer support logs?\n\u2192 Keep old embeddings in S3 Vectors\n\u2192 Keep recent ones in OpenSearch\n\u2192 Save money and keep search fast.\n\n\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n\n\ud83d\udd39 4. My take\n\nS3 Vectors is a shift: embedding storage is now part of your main data layer (S3).\nBut it\u2019s not a silver bullet \u2014 low latency still needs a real vector DB.\nThe real power lies in layered architecture + smart promotion.\n\n\ud83d\udc49 Are you already building embedding / RAG systems?\nWhich vector store are you using today, and would S3 Vectors change your setup?\n\n\ud83d\udccc Links to AWS docs in comments.\n\u2026more",
