  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.617541",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "\ud83d\ude80 3 AWS Data Engineering Tweaks That Save Time and Cost\n\n1\ufe0f\u20e3 Design partitions around query patterns\nIn Athena or Redshift Spectrum, don\u2019t just partition by date. Partition by columns you frequently filter on (e.g., region, customer_id). Cuts down scanned data = lower cost.\n\n2\ufe0f\u20e3 Prefer columnar formats everywhere\nWhether you\u2019re using Athena, Redshift, or even S3 Select \u2014 Parquet/ORC > JSON/CSV. Less I/O, faster queries, and serious cost savings.\n\n3\ufe0f\u20e3 Push compute to the engine, not your app\nRun filters/aggregations in SQL on Athena/Redshift instead of exporting raw data to Python. Engines are optimized for it \u2192 saves both time and money.\n\n\ud83d\udca1 Small tweaks, but across TB-scale data, they add up to thousands in savings.\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 733,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "1",
        "comments": "0",
        "shares": "0"
      },
