    "id": "a6ec983c-ecce-41b8-ba05-2019e2f4b810"
  },
  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.624103",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "\u201cOne of the underrated ways to deepen expertise -> review other people\u2019s work.\u201d\n\n\n\nBecoming a better engineer isn\u2019t only about writing code.\n\nLast year, I got the chance to be a technical reviewer for Data Engineering with AWS Cookbook (Packt, 2024).\n\nIt taught me 2 unexpected lessons:\n\n1\ufe0f\u20e3 Explaining > Knowing\nWhen you review someone else\u2019s technical writing, you have to test whether their logic and examples hold up for all readers. That forces you to deeply understand the material yourself.\n\n2\ufe0f\u20e3 Details matter more than we think small things \u2014 like ambiguous instructions or skipped context, but it becomes huge blockers for the reader. Reviewing made me pay more attention to clarity in my own code and documentation.\n\n3\ufe0f\u20e3 Teaching is the fastest way to learn\nReviewing wasn\u2019t just about helping an author; it sharpened my own AWS + data engineering skills.\n\n\ud83d\udc49 If you ever get a chance to teach, review, or explain\u2014grab it. It will stretch you in ways solo learning can\u2019t.\n\n(And yes, proud to have contributed to this book \ud83d\udcda).\n\n\ud83d\udccc Dropping the book link in comments if you\u2019d like to check it out.\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 1113,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "3",
        "comments": "1",
        "shares": "0"
      },
      "original_timestamp": ""
    },
    "metrics": {
      "likes": 3,
      "comments": 1
