    "id": "f7a483e1-0a7a-4395-b6cb-89f87bba160c"
  },
  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.632623",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "I read just 20 pages a day \u2014 here\u2019s what changed.\n\n\nReading used to feel like a chore for me \u2014 until I reframed it not as a goal, but as part of my growth system.\n\nHere\u2019s how the habit transformed my productivity and learning:\n\n1\ufe0f\u20e3 Micro doses, consistent rhythm\nI read 20-30 minutes daily (mostly before bed). Sometimes just 5 pages. What matters is making it regular.\n\n2\ufe0f\u20e3 Use tools to amplify retention (I have started this very recently)\n \u2022 I highlight what stands out & save quotes in Notion.\n \u2022 I revisit these notes weekly.\n \u2022 Occasionally I feed summaries into an AI tool to summarize or generate discussion questions.\n\n3\ufe0f\u20e3 Reading as mental resting / creative cross-pollination\nReading fiction, history, or non-tech books helps me recharge, see patterns differently, and bring fresh analogies to work.\n\n4\ufe0f\u20e3 Protecting reading time \u2192 boundary setting\nNo phone before bed. Weekend mornings have \u201creading first.\u201d It\u2019s as much about what you turn off as what you read.\n\n\nReading isn\u2019t just for joy\u2014it\u2019s a muscle that powers empathy, clarity, creativity, and focus.\n\n\ud83d\udc49 What book are you reading now, and how do you protect time for it?\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 1146,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "3",
        "comments": "1",
        "shares": "0"
      },
      "original_timestamp": ""
