  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.666567",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "Context switching is the silent killer of engineering productivity.\n\nAs engineers, we like to think we\u2019re good at multitasking \u2014 fixing a bug here, reviewing a PR there, jumping into a meeting mid-debug. But here\u2019s what I\u2019ve learned the hard way:\n\nMultitasking isn\u2019t productivity. It\u2019s disguised burnout.\n\nWhen you\u2019re deep in debugging a Spark job\ud83e\udd2f, and Slack pings you for a code review \u2014 your brain \ud83e\udde0 doesn\u2019t just switch. It needs ~20 minutes to re-load the mental stack you dropped. Multiply that by a day, and you lose hours \u23f1\ufe0f\n\nHere\u2019s what has helped me:\n\u2705 Batch tasks \u2192 Code reviews once in the morning, once in the evening.\n\u2705 Block time for deep work \u2192 2\u20133 hour slots, no meetings, no Slack.\n\u2705 Set \u201cresponse expectations\u201d \u2192 If people know you reply at 1pm, they won\u2019t expect instant answers.\n\u2705 Single-thread priorities \u2192 One big thing a day > 10 half-finished ones.\n\nEngineering isn\u2019t about doing more. It\u2019s about creating focus for the work that actually matters.\n\nHow do you protect your deep work time?\nDrop one hack you use to cut context switching \u2014 always looking for new ideas.\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 1099,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "1",
        "comments": "0",
        "shares": "0"
      },
      "original_timestamp": ""
    },
    "metrics": {
      "likes": 1,
      "comments": 0
    },
    "profile_id": "haymang",
    "id": "50065c64-0bf8-4f10-a904-ee01aadddea1"
  },
  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.673956",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "After years of experience across service firms, startups, and Amazon, one trait has outperformed every tool or framework: Ownership.\n\nWhat I mean by ownership:\n\ud83e\udd14 Owning the outcome, not just the task.\n\ud83e\udd14 Thinking beyond your 8-hour sprint \u2014 who will maintain this code in 6 months?\n\ud83e\udd14 Considering cost, performance, and the user impact, not only whether it \u201cworks\u201d.\n\nA few quick examples:\n\ud83d\udc49 In a service firm, ownership meant never shipping a change without a rollback plan.\n\ud83d\udc49 At a startup, it meant building a feature end-to-end because no one else could.\n\ud83d\udc49 At Amazon, ownership means being accountable for architecture, cost, and long-term operability.\n\nIf you want to accelerate your career, stop asking \u201cIs this my job?\u201d \u2014 start asking \u201cWill this still work and be owned in 6 months?\u201d The answer changes your approach.\n\n\ud83d\ude80 What\u2019s one small change you can make this week to show more ownership at work?\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 915,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "4",
        "comments": "0",
        "shares": "0"
      },
      "original_timestamp": ""
    },
    "metrics": {
      "likes": 4,
      "comments": 0
    },
    "profile_id": "haymang",
    "id": "2fd44251-6477-4ac4-a6bc-0fc94d3a33a1"
  },
  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.681903",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "\ud83e\udd16 Will AI replace data engineers?\n\n\nShort answer: No.\nBut it\u2019s already changing what being a data engineer means.\n\nWhen I started, DE was about:\n\u2022 ETL pipelines\n\u2022 Batch jobs\n\u2022 Warehouses\n\nToday, I see the shift happening in 3 areas:\n\n1. AI-Native Pipelines\n\u2192 Retrieval systems, embeddings, vector databases.\n\u2192 Think \u201cprepare data for LLMs\u201d instead of just dashboards.\n\n2. APIs & Microservices\n\u2192 Instead of just Spark jobs, DEs are building API-driven services for downstream apps.\n \n3. AI + Ops\n\u2192 Monitoring data quality for AI systems is harder than for BI. Bias, drift, hallucination\u2014new challenges.\n\nThe good news?\nThe core skills stay the same: Python, SQL, distributed systems, cloud.\nBut where we apply them keeps evolving.\n\nSo no, AI won\u2019t replace us.\nBut DEs who learn AI-era skills will replace those who don\u2019t.\n\nI expanded on that: why the change matters, the exact skills to learn, and three practical projects you can ship this quarter.\nData Engineering In The Age Of AI: https://lnkd.in/gj_zrVaX\n\n\ud83d\udc49 What\u2019s the one AI-related skill you\u2019re planning to learn this year?\n\ud83d\udc49 What project would you pick first \u2014 RAG, real-time enrichment, or cost optimization?\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 1175,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "16",
        "comments": "0",
        "shares": "0"
      },
      "original_timestamp": ""
    },
    "metrics": {
      "likes": 16,
      "comments": 0
