    "profile_id": "haymang",
    "id": "ed72f5df-1d52-454a-8cca-c31410b2caa7"
  },
  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.688811",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "\ud83d\udccc How I study 2 hrs/day while working full-time at Amazon\n\nFor years, I thought I didn\u2019t have time to learn new skills.\nBut here\u2019s the truth: I didn\u2019t have a system.\n\nToday, even while working full-time, I consistently dedicate ~2 hrs/day to learning.\nHere\u2019s my simple system:\n\n1. Time Blocking\n\u2192 1 hr in the morning before work, 1 hr at night. Non-negotiable.\n\n2. One Theme per Quarter\n\u2192 Instead of chasing 5 tools at once, I pick 1 area (e.g., distributed systems, APIs, or AI pipelines).\n\n3. Micro-Projects > Courses\n\u2192 Instead of endless tutorials, I build something tiny but real. Example: A log parser with Python, a mini API in FastAPI.\n \n4. Consistency > Intensity\n\u2192 Even 45 focused minutes beat a random 5-hr binge session.\n\nThe result? \nOver the last 2 years, I\u2019ve:\n\u2705 Transitioned from pure data engineering \u2192 software development\n\u2705 Built side projects in GenAI + backend\n\u2705 Stayed ahead in a field that changes every 6 months\n\nIf you\u2019re stuck in \u201cno time to learn\u201d mode, try this system.\nIt compounds.\n\n\ud83d\udc49 How do you manage learning outside work?\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 1062,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "123",
        "comments": "14",
        "shares": "0"
      },
      "original_timestamp": ""
    },
    "metrics": {
      "likes": 123,
      "comments": 14
