    "profile_id": "haymang",
    "id": "9f14d3f3-1bda-4856-8f4a-294ff958ce6e"
  },
  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.695747",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "The tools will keep changing. But the fundamentals? They stay.\n\nIn 11+ years as a data engineer, I\u2019ve seen the hype cycles\u2014new frameworks every month, new \u201cmust-learn\u201d tools every year.\n\nBut the truth is \u2192 the engineers who thrive are the ones who master the fundamentals. Tools come and go. Core skills compound.\n\nHere are the 5 skills I\u2019d double down on as we are almost at the end of 2025 \ud83d\udc47\n\n \u2014\u2014\u2014\n\n1. SQL (Seriously)\nDon\u2019t sleep on SQL. Writing optimized queries, complex joins, and reading execution plans\u2014these separate junior from senior. Whether you\u2019re in Snowflake, Redshift, BigQuery, or the latest booming ClickHouse, SQL is the foundation.\n\n2. Python\nAutomation, APIs, orchestration\u2014Python is everywhere. Get comfortable not just with scripts, but also libraries like pandas, FastAPI, and typing.\n\n3. Cloud Architecture\nData engineers today should know how the cloud works\u2014IAM, networking, storage classes, and cost models. It\u2019s no longer optional.\n\n4. Distributed Systems\nData is too big for one machine. Spark for processing, Kafka for streaming\u2014understanding partitioning, shuffling, and backpressure makes you 10x better at solving scale problems.\n\n5. DevOps Mindset\nGit, CI/CD, Docker, IaC\u2014our field is merging with software engineering. The ability to version, test, and deploy your work is a career booster.\n\n \u2e3b\n\n\ud83d\udca1 Bonus skills:\n \u2022 APIs & microservices\n \u2022 Data governance & observability\n \u2022 GenAI + vector databases (coming fast)\n\n \u2e3b\n\n\ud83d\udc49 Which of these skills are you focusing on this year? Or struggling with? Drop it below\u2014I\u2019ll point you to resources or share how I learned it.\n\u2026more",
