  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.711292",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "Tech changes fast.\nBut chasing every trend? That\u2019s a fast track to burnout.\n.\n.\n.\nIn 11+ years, I\u2019ve seen buzzwords come and go.\nBig data, real-time, cloud-first, serverless, GenAI, LLMs\u2014each one brought a wave of hype and pressure to \u201ccatch up or fall behind.\u201d\n\nEarly in my career, I tried to keep up with everything.\nI bookmarked every article, signed up for 10 newsletters, jumped between courses\u2014and felt like I was learning nothing deeply.\n\nNow, I approach it differently.\n\n\u2e3b\n\nHere\u2019s my system to stay sharp without losing my mind:\n\n1. One Deep Dive Per Month\nEach month, I pick one area to explore\u2014whether it\u2019s vector databases, Kubernetes internals, or GenAI APIs.\nThe goal is to build something with it\u2014not just read about it.\n\n2. Filtered Information Diet\n \u2022 Newsletters I actually read:\n \u2022 Data Engineering Weekly\n \u2022 tl;dr Data\n \u2022 The Pragmatic Engineer\n \u2022 YouTube > Courses: 10-minute focused videos help me decide if a topic\u2019s worth diving into further.\n\n3. Build to Learn\nInstead of trying to finish full Udemy or Coursera courses, I build mini projects:\n \u2022 A FastAPI wrapper around a GenAI service\n \u2022 A Spark job to process simulated IoT data\n \u2022 A local pipeline using DuckDB + Python + Streamlit\n\nYou\u2019ll retain more by building than watching.\n \n4. Say No (on purpose)\nI ignore 90% of trends. Unless it directly improves how I build, ship, or think\u2014I don\u2019t care.\nThat\u2019s not laziness. That\u2019s focus.\n\n\u2e3b\n\nReminder:\nYou don\u2019t need to learn everything.\nYou need to learn what moves the needle in your work.\n\n\n\n\nWhat\u2019s your learning system?\nOr what do you struggle with most\u2014focus, time, or deciding what to learn next?\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 1633,
