  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.727039",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "Choosing between a service firm, startup, or Big Tech?\nHere\u2019s what actually changes\u2014and what doesn\u2019t.\n\n\n\nHaving worked in all three, let me break down what really shifts\u2014and how to decide based on where you are in your career.\n\n\u2e3b\n\nService-Based Companies\n\n\u2022 Work style: Structured, process-heavy, often working with enterprise clients.\n\u2022 Tech stack: Usually mature systems; you\u2019ll use stable, proven tech, and work with batch pipelines, SQL, Java, etc.\n\u2022 Pace: Slower\u2014but don\u2019t mistake that for easy.\n\u2022 Pressure: Very high. Mistakes can cost real money. Systems are critical, and there\u2019s no room for \u201cwe\u2019ll fix it in prod.\u201d\n\u2022 Best for: Learning discipline, working in big teams, getting exposure to enterprise systems and formal SDLC.\n\nWhat I learned:\nPrecision, accountability, and how to work in complex org setups. I once spent two weeks tuning a single Spark job that failed silently in prod because a batch ran out of memory under edge conditions. You don\u2019t forget those lessons.\n\n\u2e3b\n\nStartups\n\n\u2022 Work style: Fast, messy, you wear every hat.\n\u2022 Tech stack: Mix of modern and scrappy\u2014use what works, not what\u2019s \u201cright.\u201d\n\u2022 Pace: Insane. You\u2019re shipping real features in days.\n\u2022 Pressure: High, but different. There\u2019s room to break things\u2014but no time to overthink.\n\u2022 Best for: Building speed, confidence, ownership. If you want to learn a lot fast\u2014startups are bootcamps in disguise.\n\nWhat I learned:\nSpeed, resilience, and bias for action. At one point, I built, deployed, and supported a customer-facing analytics dashboard in a week\u2014with zero QA team. You learn fast when the users are already in prod.\n\n\u2e3b\n\nBig Tech\n\n\u2022 Work style: Fast but controlled. You move quickly\u2014but at scale, with strong safety nets.\n\u2022 Tech stack: Deep. You touch infra, services, data, AI\u2014often all in one stack.\n\u2022 Pace: Balanced. There\u2019s urgency, but things are done with long-term impact in mind.\n\u2022 Pressure: High. Millions use your stuff. You can\u2019t \u201cjust patch it later.\u201d\n\u2022 Best for: Working on world-class systems, learning from senior devs, understanding design at scale.\n\nWhat I learned:\nDesigning systems that are secure, scalable, and auditable. I helped build GenAI apps on sensitive internal data\u2014privacy, latency, and compliance all mattered. It\u2019s where depth meets responsibility.\n\n\u2e3b\n\nWhat doesn\u2019t change?\n \u2022 You\u2019ll always need to solve problems.\n \u2022 You\u2019ll always be learning.\n \u2022 The fundamentals matter everywhere.\n\n\nAre you at a crossroads in your career? Thinking of switching company types?\nTell me where you\u2019re headed\u2014and I\u2019ll share what to look out for.\n\u2026more",
