  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.750310",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "The tools will keep changing. But the core skills? They stay.\n\n.\n.\n.\n\n\nThere\u2019s a lot of noise in the data world. Every week, there\u2019s a new tool, a new framework, or a \u201cmust-learn\u201d course. But after 11+ years in this space, here\u2019s what I\u2019ve learned:\n\nMaster the fundamentals\u2014and you\u2019ll adapt to anything.\n\nHere are the core skills I believe every data engineer should double down on in 2025:\n\n\u2e3b\n\n1. SQL (Seriously)\nDon\u2019t sleep on SQL. Writing complex joins, optimizing queries, understanding execution plans\u2014these still separate junior from senior. Whether you\u2019re in Snowflake, Redshift, or BigQuery, SQL is your foundation.\n\n\u2e3b\n\n2. Python\nScripting, automation, APIs, orchestration\u2014Python is everywhere. I use it daily, whether it\u2019s for data manipulation or backend service logic. Bonus: get comfortable with pandas, FastAPI, and typing.\n\n\u2e3b\n\n3. Cloud Architecture\nYou need to know how the cloud works. Data engineers today should be able to spin up a data pipeline on AWS/GCP, know what costs what, and understand things like IAM, networking, and storage classes.\n\n\u2e3b\n\n4. Distributed Systems (e.g., Spark, Kafka)\nData\u2019s too big to run on one machine. Whether it\u2019s Spark for processing or Kafka for streaming, knowing how distributed processing works\u2014shuffling, partitioning, backpressure\u2014makes you 10x better at solving problems.\n\n\u2e3b\n\n5. DevOps Mindset\nGit. CI/CD. Docker. Infra as code. As data engineering blends more with software development, versioning and deployment skills are no longer optional.\n\n\u2e3b\n\nOptional but game-changing:\n \u2022 APIs & microservices\n \u2022 Data governance & observability\n \u2022 GenAI + vector DB basics (it\u2019s coming fast)\n\n\nWhich skill are you focusing on this year? Or struggling with? Drop it below\u2014I\u2019ll point you to resources or share how I learned it.\n\n\nhashtag\n#DataEngineering \nhashtag\n#GenAi \nhashtag\n#SoftwareDevelopment\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 1851,
      "engagement": {
