  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.757975",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "I spent years building data pipelines. But I wanted more than pipelines\u2014I wanted products \n\nAs a data engineer, I loved solving infrastructure problems. Designing scalable data pipelines, optimizing Spark jobs, tuning performance across massive datasets\u2014it was deep work, and I got good at it \ud83d\ude07\n\nBut over time, something kept bothering me \ud83d\ude10\n\nI was enabling others to build products\u2014but not building them myself \ud83d\ude12\n\nThat\u2019s when I started leaning into software development. Not because DE wasn\u2019t valuable\u2014but because I wanted to be part of the full picture. I didn\u2019t just want to move data from A to B. I wanted to own the user-facing application, the APIs, the services, the whole experience.\n\nHere\u2019s what changed:\n \u2022 Mindset: I went from thinking in terms of jobs and DAGs to thinking in services, systems, and APIs.\n \u2022 Skills: I doubled down on design principles, clean architecture, and backend engineering.\n \u2022 Impact: I now build end-to-end apps, many powered by GenAI, used across teams at Amazon. I don\u2019t just build data infra\u2014I build solutions.\n\nWas it hard? \ud83e\udee8\nYes. Transitioning wasn\u2019t just about picking up a new language. It meant letting go of familiar tools, starting from scratch in areas like distributed systems, service ownership, and large-scale architecture.\n\nBut it\u2019s been 100% worth it. \n\nBecause now, when I build something\u2014it doesn\u2019t just end at the warehouse. It reaches the customer \ud83e\udd73\n\nAre you a data engineer thinking of expanding into software dev? Or already made the switch? Drop your questions or share your story. It\u2019s always worth hearing every single journey.\n\u2026more",
      "hashtags": [],
      "length": 1600,
      "engagement": {
        "likes": "18",
        "comments": "2",
