  {
    "timestamp": "2026-04-08T23:59:23.766016",
    "event_type": "historical_post",
    "content": {
      "post_content": "11 years. 3 types of companies. 1 consistent lesson: \nNothing beats ownership\n\nWhen I look back on my career\u2014from Infosys to Amazon\u2014the contrast between the types of companies I\u2019ve worked at stands out more than anything else. Each one taught me a different aspect of engineering, discipline, and problem-solving.\n\n1. Service-based company (Infosys):\nThis is where it all began. Think heavy batch jobs, traditional ETL pipelines, and regulated environments. You typically work on client-defined problems, and while timelines are usually clear and steady, there\u2019s zero room for error. A small bug in production could impact billing systems or critical reports for Fortune 500 clients. So, the margin for experimentation is thin, but you gain sharpness in debugging, discipline, and accountability.\n\n2. Startup world:\nNow flip that. Suddenly, I wasn\u2019t just building; I was designing, deploying, testing, monitoring\u2014everything. The environment was fast-paced, chaotic, and incredibly empowering. You don\u2019t wait to be told what to do\u2014you figure it out. I once built a real-time pipeline to handle location-based user events in under 10 days because there was no one else to do it. Startups teach you speed, bias for action, and how to deliver when everything is on fire.\n\n3. Amazon (Tech Giant):\nThis was a different beast. Amazon is a blend of the above: velocity and scale. You\u2019re still expected to move fast\u2014but now you\u2019re dealing with systems that serve millions. One small mistake doesn\u2019t just break a report\u2014it can take down regional infrastructure. Ownership here means being deeply accountable\u2014not just for code, but for architecture, cost, performance, and long-term maintainability. I\u2019ve been here 5 years now, and every day I still learn.\n\nAcross all three environments, the biggest common factor has been ownership. Whether it\u2019s a 20-line script or a 20-service system, you\u2019ve got to think like the system\u2019s success is your own.\n\nWhere are you in your journey\u2014service firm, startup, or big tech? What\u2019s been your biggest takeaway so far?\n\n\nhashtag\n#DataEngineering \nhashtag\n#BeCurious\n\u2026more",
